#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128979 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "2f8542a8-1e9e-56f8-9eae-e5b4fb592d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5965" :question/problem "Find the family of surfaces that describe the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y, z) = \\ln(x^2 + 2y^2 - z^2).$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{c}}+\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{{e}^{c}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{e}^{c}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of hyperboloids of one sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{e^c} + \\dfrac{y^2}{e^c/2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{e^c} =  1, \\qquad c \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128980 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "6a7316fa-77fe-584f-bd92-a37521371482" :question/math-academy-id "e-5966" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z) = \\sin{xyz}$ that passes through the point $P\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n  f\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)&= \\sin \\left(\\pi\\cdot 1 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} = 1}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡xyz & =1 \\\\ xyz & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$ is the surface $xyz= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2\\pi k,$ where $k$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128981 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "397d8dee-c35f-5908-baa6-002e09dab477" :question/math-academy-id "e-5967" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+4y^2-z,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z = x^2+\\dfrac{y^2}{{1/4}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c86359-b5f2-514d-be3f-7b8adf667285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259924} {:answer/id #uuid "be5bca04-521d-507b-9605-3ed467bfb3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261552} {:answer/id #uuid "beb6569c-e1af-5e70-80d8-340b15cfb0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4x-y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261558} {:answer/id #uuid "db925ce6-b9c0-5c85-adc3-53cc5edcc3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4x+y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261816} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ca3d79-e9ac-5159-9580-76796581523b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be5bca04-521d-507b-9605-3ed467bfb3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261552} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c60c78f-31b0-59a0-a0fc-3c728fd05ebc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "072141c1-a125-53a1-b9ae-dcc217a41236" :question/math-academy-id "q-129182" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=z-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1 & =1 \\\\ z & =4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dd6f7b6-9fba-5e5a-b885-947e8862ee65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380749} {:answer/id #uuid "24dce5c8-935a-5b1f-b728-a2be7d268a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380750} {:answer/id #uuid "6873c5f4-923c-5bf4-b140-e8e4a5929650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380751} {:answer/id #uuid "89f9df36-c487-5f54-8b1f-aba713a4bef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380752} {:answer/id #uuid "3866b711-9fa7-5bca-aa25-f6d2aeb49943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24dce5c8-935a-5b1f-b728-a2be7d268a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380750} :answer-field/id #uuid "044bd578-4705-5aa8-80b4-fc8f6a1c787c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "288a5fbe-4296-58f2-a71d-edcf3f852c57" :question/math-academy-id "q-129184" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 2z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the paraboloid $z=\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f54afa7-92b9-552b-9928-46fb97c4d49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260145} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4dcc43-25c1-5b28-a98b-8c2818c51646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260417} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbdb8c0-715d-516d-b690-eb05aabc64d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260854} {:answer/id #uuid "7af27507-8bb6-5aa4-be14-0580e6493659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=4{x}^{2}+36{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260947} {:answer/id #uuid "cb31943f-b1cd-5ab0-a60b-ab38872c9029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d4dcc43-25c1-5b28-a98b-8c2818c51646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260417} :answer-field/id #uuid "27308b93-8f8c-50b8-9823-f11e329d7ea5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bf976be-dc2f-5638-a981-b519d9bcc78f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129194" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point $P(3,1,6).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,1,6) & =\\frac{{6}^{2}}{{3}^{2}+9({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{18} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}) \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(3,1,6)$ is the elliptic cone\n ${z}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffc4b44b-454f-562b-a12c-dc2a52ba993b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of planes: $z=\\frac{c}{3}x+2cy$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400396} {:answer/id #uuid "d402b0a2-89d4-5271-aa83-0fc1a61cc42a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces: $z=\\frac{3c}{x+6y}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400397} {:answer/id #uuid "80d5b36d-2d40-5c18-8e0d-6d105a433548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of planes: $z=3cx+\\frac{c}{2}y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400398} {:answer/id #uuid "5d402e8a-f439-5606-a630-6e0174166674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of planes: $z=\\frac{c}{3}x+\\frac{c}{2}y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400399} {:answer/id #uuid "21b57ee1-26b9-5c0c-82c9-e946f819e503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces: $z=\\frac{c}{3(x+6y)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffc4b44b-454f-562b-a12c-dc2a52ba993b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of planes: $z=\\frac{c}{3}x+2cy$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400396} :answer-field/id #uuid "a82caa02-cf2e-5fd0-9527-3a7c2077a7da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b42698a-11e2-5e4e-8c95-f9d962f16e6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-129197" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{3z}{x+6y}?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{3z}{x+6y} & =c \\\\ 3z & =c(x+6y) \\\\ 3z & =cx+6cy \\\\ z & =\\frac{c}{3}x+2cy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of planes:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{c}{3}x+2cy,\\,c\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3865a270-9ab3-58ae-8649-0efa8982fdce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces: $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3)+c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260375} {:answer/id #uuid "5f5e8b66-96bf-50b8-b97f-b8ab5f43ff15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z=-4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}+3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260696} {:answer/id #uuid "9486e61c-2e27-5fa9-8077-fb48b904dcdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261169} {:answer/id #uuid "aee98f0a-20b0-5bd6-a537-aff0c9a292f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces: $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3-c)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261417} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a5a625-3607-5439-834c-26063d8cdf57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of hyperboloids of two sheets: ${z}^{2}=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9486e61c-2e27-5fa9-8077-fb48b904dcdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261169} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a47eeb-4baa-5b18-abe5-9f7cd72e0cf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d43deba6-d25d-5be9-ac38-00c06f2c958a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129199" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-z-3}?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-z-3} & =c \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-z-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ z & =4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c>0.$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of elliptic paraboloids:\n\n$$\nz=4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c,\\,c\\in (0,\\infty )\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dcea0d4-0aa7-599b-b4a3-6c92b0f35ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by  ${z}^{2}+c={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $c>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321631} {:answer/id #uuid "66821ea6-4a15-56c1-8de5-1cde68434201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by ${z}^{2}+c={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $c\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321945} {:answer/id #uuid "6a381616-6113-5d71-a8ce-f042c87ca906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of spheres centered at $(0,0,{e}^{-1})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321976} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb79478-f7ab-570b-9c5a-183970102885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of spheres centered at the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321993} {:answer/id #uuid "d208c461-ff49-5023-94f5-df68a82be0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of spheres centered at $(0,0,e)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cb79478-f7ab-570b-9c5a-183970102885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of spheres centered at the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321993} :answer-field/id #uuid "05d519a4-998d-5a7e-b664-c9b0f2e8613f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a64b6d6-ab0a-5726-932f-2f26ae11023b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36342" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} & =c \\\\ -{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $0<c\\le 1$ since $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c$ must be non-negative. If we let $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c={r}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surfaces form a family of spheres centered at the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0165b2e6-32cf-5ed6-892f-a62ffa739bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}={3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321163} {:answer/id #uuid "2cdc6e2e-a009-5e1e-ab37-64419fc6c51a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y+3z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321495} {:answer/id #uuid "6b273a58-a5c1-50f5-ae32-78f397aaea4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y+3z+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321983} {:answer/id #uuid "88ab13f1-deeb-5220-91b6-63ba4002bb8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322238} {:answer/id #uuid "d892add4-43a1-53ef-8e4e-e1741fd17258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y+3z-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d892add4-43a1-53ef-8e4e-e1741fd17258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y+3z-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322843} :answer-field/id #uuid "887bc4c8-1fd3-50f6-a073-6544abe02c46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96466546-d66d-5bfb-9320-7db7a3c30bde" :question/math-academy-id "q-36986" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2y+3z)$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2(1)+3(0))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2y+3z) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x+2y+3z & =3 \\\\ x+2y+3z-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,0)$ is the plane $x+2y+3z-3=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07bae223-68a9-5961-9914-da375ccce6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508399} {:answer/id #uuid "1eaf098d-4f19-555f-9faf-939cea3c6583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{x+1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508441} {:answer/id #uuid "88df6524-28d0-527e-835e-f94d1cb3d8aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{{e}^{x+1}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508594} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ff3e45-de27-5738-a884-ad437f51e2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{{e}^{2\\sqrt{x+1}}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508700} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4da4e5-c2d2-5952-a370-830c063e72c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={e}^{2\\sqrt{x+1}-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6ff3e45-de27-5738-a884-ad437f51e2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{{e}^{2\\sqrt{x+1}}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508700} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d48eaeb-3099-53dc-b2bb-59ac6532ac96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2111d3e1-e583-5d4e-9209-d92970f2922b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36992" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz})-\\sqrt{x+1},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz})-\\sqrt{x+1} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz}) & =\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{yz} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}} \\\\ yz & ={({e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}})}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\sqrt{x+1}}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the surface $z=\\frac{{e}^{2\\sqrt{x+1}}}{y}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d7bb95b-e81b-5290-962a-70871869c5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321626} {:answer/id #uuid "a0158118-ac50-5577-8261-27c0a8097cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322413} {:answer/id #uuid "d9609566-facb-5a23-9e36-ce3058d351f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322850} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd980e2-08ed-5db7-bbae-19f2c480ab5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322899} {:answer/id #uuid "e2bf068e-ec9c-5fc8-954b-3ba3cf0b76a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0158118-ac50-5577-8261-27c0a8097cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322413} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8392a62-a699-5691-81bb-f77e4ec364c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "749b0ce1-41bf-572e-a7ea-efb60d5412a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37131" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z$ that passes through the point $P(0,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1,1) & =0\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ xy+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-xy \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-xy) \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(0,1,1)$ is the surface $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "091f3be1-5140-515d-94a7-0a29922c8024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336109} {:answer/id #uuid "5582cc4b-bdc3-544b-a70e-dc7b63d64a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336817} {:answer/id #uuid "5d069d46-77ff-58a4-8813-38b5932e5ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336875} {:answer/id #uuid "5f5d95ba-9657-5291-863a-bc5ec9a54194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336896} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7f3d5a-6791-5d6b-9ac1-81f3f01b91dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "091f3be1-5140-515d-94a7-0a29922c8024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336109} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3e82e8c-f371-5e01-91cc-a90ca566ba4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e769fbd-3008-51d0-9273-bebdb60503ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-71572" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+z-2,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+z-2 & =-2 \\\\ z & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=-2$ is the hyperbolic paraboloid $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54665d21-bcc9-5a95-91e2-77f90b5d0484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=x+y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336812} {:answer/id #uuid "6a957d23-d58c-5e8e-9d75-b84e41fd26d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{1}{x+y-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336975} {:answer/id #uuid "c3323711-c540-55cf-b9d8-00f353eb0f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=x+y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337765} {:answer/id #uuid "e016822e-a7af-5692-802d-f76e4c8b3920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=x-y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337991} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7af7c7-d682-5aa7-a3a6-61ebc6796f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-x-y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3323711-c540-55cf-b9d8-00f353eb0f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=x+y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337765} :answer-field/id #uuid "f694fda3-9767-50a9-8b7f-15ec1151fd51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1df136f5-69d9-502e-8d2a-b817d7b3a8f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-71574" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=z-x-y-1,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-x-y-1 & =1 \\\\ z & =x+y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the plane $z=x+y+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "073764a6-4153-5fe6-b84d-4c9ca0a950a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by $3c{x}^{2}-c{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508398} {:answer/id #uuid "6ddf0199-8b66-5e41-b0ea-4bdf1a9d0e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $3c{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2}=z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508554} {:answer/id #uuid "8d268ae2-5c04-5477-bdf6-af3c935a1550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by $3c{x}^{2}-c{y}^{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508604} {:answer/id #uuid "d194602d-cc7c-51ec-a4ff-52ca3453f17d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by $3c{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508722} {:answer/id #uuid "fd32e248-1201-5b37-b595-0f0420859b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of hyperboloids of one sheet: $3c{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d194602d-cc7c-51ec-a4ff-52ca3453f17d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by $3c{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508722} :answer-field/id #uuid "113c6c84-a326-59a4-aca0-106d9621d960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95921cae-0a0b-5427-8528-229b03960d35" :question/math-academy-id "q-71584" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ c(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =1 \\\\ 3c{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)$ can't be zero or negative. Therefore, we get a family of surfaces given by\n\n$$\n3c{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2}=1,\\,c\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2d0a3e-f37a-5951-b416-009e6796a144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of planes: $z=cx+cy$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382168} {:answer/id #uuid "a10b4244-f123-532e-b911-1596c17d9177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(c(x+y))$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382169} {:answer/id #uuid "32c572a4-4f7c-5d03-bc5c-7bd8ef33130c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by $z=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(c(x+y))$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382170} {:answer/id #uuid "993c0e73-e553-524c-ad84-c340fa775dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by $z=c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382171} {:answer/id #uuid "732828ca-06c4-54ee-bc9a-50a3e0ecfe19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(c(x+y))$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a10b4244-f123-532e-b911-1596c17d9177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(c(x+y))$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382169} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4a8ee9-2814-57a9-b6c3-40d0efdb506f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "919edf36-e85a-586e-b028-964f61fdbd59" :question/math-academy-id "q-71590" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z}{x+y}?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z}{x+y} & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z & =c(x+y) \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(c(x+y))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of surfaces given by:\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(c(x+y)),\\,c\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c5ddfd-1f89-51f0-967f-6991ca9b14f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336211} {:answer/id #uuid "161287b5-4c83-5620-96d7-a6e4eae1183f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}-2xy-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336239} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac3c569-8ebc-5f0f-be7f-d0a7507f724b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=(x+y)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336977} {:answer/id #uuid "a5157b26-146f-5a95-931b-c4b87daa8788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337533} {:answer/id #uuid "afd12020-f92c-57bc-87cf-919b87cc136e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=(x-y)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5157b26-146f-5a95-931b-c4b87daa8788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337533} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b3b081d-731c-5f19-ad01-39dea5ef81b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9bc3409b-e78c-5e8f-bfcf-f26157077219" :question/math-academy-id "q-71593" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{xy}$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,2) & =\\frac{(2)-(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}{(1)(1)}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{z-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{xy} & =2 \\\\ z-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2xy \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,2)$ is the surface $z={x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7161819e-ecce-539e-a7d9-dd1392ee66bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409905} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6e5c59-e63b-5d91-add0-3fc776b81f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409908} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e2ddba-6b1f-50a3-8f82-6a0e5406e403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409913} {:answer/id #uuid "f41b80f9-1883-5d0f-a39d-6bf74d468448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409914} {:answer/id #uuid "fab3e1ad-3d44-56bd-8a1c-54fb07d88d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7161819e-ecce-539e-a7d9-dd1392ee66bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409905} :answer-field/id #uuid "e10467a3-d69e-5402-aad3-4b64333f090a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "595252cd-1afe-5f16-ae97-13c476966ff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71594" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}$ that passes through the point $P(2,\\sqrt{5},3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,\\sqrt{5},3) & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2(2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}-(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(2,\\sqrt{5},3)$ is the surface ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0.$"}]}
